# NAT/masquerade soak policy — the gap the 48 h soak of 2026-08-08 did
# not cover. That policy exercised counters, rate limiting, sampled
# logging, conntrack and a drop matrix; it contained no NAT at all,
# while masquerade is the entire function of the office deployment.
#
# The return-path rule is `allow if pkt.proto == tcp`, NOT
# `allow if conntrack(pkt).state == established`, because the stateful
# form does not work behind masquerade: the conntrack lookup lives in
# the program prelude and the de-NAT pass runs after it, so a reply is
# looked up under the address it carries on the wire while the entry
# was created under the guest's pre-translation address. Measured on
# the rig: l11_04_masq_established_reply.sh. This policy therefore
# soaks the configuration that actually forwards today.
zone lan = [enp1s0f1]
zone wanz = [enp1s0f2]

@xdp(lan)

count nat_total
count nat_egress if pkt.src_ip in 10.99.61.0/24
count nat_reply if pkt.src_ip == 10.99.71.9
count nat_est if conntrack(pkt).state == established
count nat_mcast if pkt.dst_ip in 224.0.0.0/4
log(sample=100) if pkt.proto == udp and pkt.dst_port == 9999
drop if pkt.dst_ip in 224.0.0.0/4
masquerade if pkt.src_ip in 10.99.61.0/24
dnat to 10.99.61.50:8080 if pkt.proto == tcp and pkt.dst_port == 8080
redirect to wanz if pkt.proto == udp and pkt.dst_port == 1
drop if pkt.src_ip in 10.99.60.0/24
       limited by rate_limit(500, per=src_ip)
allow if pkt.proto == tcp
allow if pkt.proto == udp and pkt.dst_port == 9999
count nat_dropped
default drop
